addresses the remaining display options. I'm referencing the SRD requirement IDs so the stitch agent can look up the exact specifications for each task.\n\nI need to verify the weight constraints though—I don't have access to the requirements.yaml file, so I'm assuming each requirement has a default weight of 1, which would keep all four tasks within the 4-point limit. Let me check if I need to read the actual weights file to confirm this assumption.\n\nLooking at the requirement structure from the context, I can see how the SRD groups break down: the first group has 5 sub-requirements, the second has 8, the third has 3, and the fourth has 2. This gives me a sense of the scope, but without the actual weights, I'll proceed with the default assumption and note that constraint.\n\nThe pattern I'm noticing is interesting—srd070-fmt has mostly single sub-requirements per group, which aligns with what was mentioned about weight-4 requirements being restructured. That suggests single-item groups might carry higher weights, but I'm working with incomplete information here. The safest approach is to assume uniform weight 1 across all sub-requirements and document that assumption clearly.\n\nNow I'm looking back at the dirname task structure to understand how to frame these properly. The naming convention follows a specific pattern: the stitch agent needs a clear task description that references the SRD, the requirement range, and what needs to be implemented. For du, I should write self-contained task descriptions that give the stitch agent everything needed to generate the code without requiring additional context.\n\nSince these tasks build sequentially—the first creates main.go and subsequent ones modify it—I need to be explicit about dependencies. For the required_reading, the stitch agent will need the SRD, the project guidelines, and any existing pkg code that du depends on like sys and format utilities. Let me finalize the task set now.\n\nActually, I should reference