ules—sys is already implemented but format isn't yet, which limits what I can propose. Let me finalize my analysis and write out the YAML with four du tasks, each covering four sub-requirements with proper dependency chains. out the contract requirement to pkg/ packages with actual exported APIs, since cmd packages are just executables with a main function and don't have meaningful public interfaces to contract.\n\nSo my final plan is: start with rel01.3 (du command), skip the contract task since it's a cmd/ package, and proceed directly to implementation tasks for the du functionality and any supporting libraries it needs.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the du implementation into four focused tasks covering the core traversal logic, size calculation options, directory traversal controls, and output formatting. Let me check the recent commit patterns to make sure I'm naming these tasks correctly.\n\nLooking at the git history, the format is consistent: `[stitch] cmd/<utility>: <description> (srd<NNN>-<utility> R<X>.<Y>-R<X>.<Z>)`. I'll apply this to my du tasks, mapping each one to its specific requirement ranges. Now I'm drafting the full YAML with these properly formatted task titles.\n\nFor the required_reading section, I need to point the stitch agent to the SRD for du requirements, the main.go file to understand what's already implemented, and pkg/sys since du relies on file metadata operations. The design decisions should come from the architecture—using pkg/sys for stat/lstat calls and pkg/format for human-readable output formatting. I'm settling on the required_reading sections now — for the initial file creation task, I'll point to the SRD, the sys package for imports, and CLAUDE.md, then for the modification tasks I'll reference the SRD and the main.go file that gets created. I should also consider pulling in the test suite and use case specs if they exist to give the stitch agent full context, though I'm working with what I can reasonably infer about the file structure. Let me finalize the output with the du implementation paths in mind. with `codereadiness: none` for rel01.3 and `codestatus: not started` for `rel01.3-uc001-du`. So there's definitely no existing code for du.\n\nLet me also think about whether the stitch agent needs to know about `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd009-du.yaml` specifically or if I need to provide the weights info. The task step 3b says to check weights. Since I don't have the weights file, I'll note my assumption.\n\nBut actually, the weights are embedded in the SRD requirements, typically in a `requirements.yaml` or within the SRD itself. The stitch constitution probably handles this. I just need to ensure the total weight per task doesn't exceed 4.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the requirements across four tasks, assuming each requirement has a weight of 1. Task 0 covers R1.1 through R1.4, Task 1 covers R1.5 and R2.1 through R2.3, Task 2 covers R2.4 through R2.7, and Task 3 covers R2.8 and R3.1 through R3.3. Each task totals exactly 4 weight units, so they're all within budget.\n\nThe only caveat is if any of these requirements actually carry a higher weight than 1, the distribution would need to shift, but without access to the actual weight values I'm working with the assumption that they're all equal.\n\nNow let me put together the response. I'll organize it into a project summary, break down the priority analysis, walk through the weight budget, and then provide the YAML task list.\n\n---\n\n## Project State Summary\n\ngo-unix-utils is reimplementing over 111 GNU coreutils and moreutils utilities in Go, with differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries to ensure parity. The pipeline is specification-driven: SRDs define what needs to be built, an orchestrator generates the Go code, and differential tests validate correctness.\n\nThe codebase is organized with individual utility packages under `cmd/`, shared libraries in `pkg/` for syscalls, formatting, and testing utilities, and specification documents in `docs/specs/`. \n\nCurrent implementation covers the syscall abstractions, hash utilities, size parsing, encoding utilities, and a handful of basic utilities like sponge, true, false, yes, basename, and dirname. The formatting library and differential testing harness haven't been started yet, and most of the remaining utilities are still pending implementation.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nLooking at the earliest incomplete release, rel00.0 is partially done but the format and testutils packages that it depends on aren't in the requirement states, so I can't propose them as tasks. The next release, rel01.1, needs cat, but that SRD also isn't showing up in the requirement states, which means I can't add it either.\n\nThat leaves rel01.3 with du as the first viable target — all 19 requirements are marked ready, the code hasn't been started, and du only depends on pkg/sys which is already implemented. The format package isn't in scope for this batch, so that's not blocking.\n\n## Weight Budget Analysis\n\nAssuming each requirement defaults to weight 1 since the weights file isn't available, I can fit the du requirements into two tasks of 4 units each. Task 0 takes the first four requirements, and Task 1 takes the next four, with the remaining requirements distributed across subsequent tasks to stay within the 4-unit budget per task.\n\nNow I need to write the YAML output with clear, actionable task descriptions for the stitch agent to follow.\n\nLooking at the architecture dependencies, the du utility depends on the sys and format packages, so I should include those APIs in the required_reading along with the SRD. For the initial task creating du from scratch, the stitch agent will need the SRD specification and references to those upstream package APIs to understand what's available. Now I'm extracting the key design decisions from the SRD and architecture—installing the SIGPIPE handler at startup, importing the sys package for file metadata