n't been implemented yet. For the basic traversal tasks, I probably only need sys for file stat operations, so I'll hold off on format until later requirements that actually use the human-readable size formatting. Since I'm writing these tasks in advance and format's contract is known, I'll include it in the required_reading for those later tasks, but for now I'll keep the early tasks focused on just what they need.\n\nLooking at the SRD structure, the grouping pattern is pretty clear: R1 covers core functionality, R2 handles options and flags, R3 deals with output formatting, R4 is version and help, and R5 catches edge cases. For du specifically, having 5 items in R1 and 8 in R2 makes sense given how many flags the command supports.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the final task definitions in YAML format.\n\nI need to think through what acceptance criteria work best for code deliverables — typically that means the build succeeds, linting passes, requirements are implemented, and behavior matches the spec.\n\nBefore I finalize all four tasks, I should also consider whether tee would be a better candidate than one of the current options.  Tee is simpler with fewer requirements, and it doesn't depend on the unimplemented format package like du does. But the constraint is clear: I should focus on the earliest incomplete release, which means du from rel01.3 takes priority over tee from rel02.1, even though du has that format dependency issue.\n\nThe P1 rule about mixing releases doesn't apply here since tee and du don't share functionality. So I'm committing to du—four tasks covering 16 of the 19 requirements. Now I'm structuring the output to give the stitch agent everything it needs: what the utility does, which requirements to implement, what files to work with, the architectural constraints, and how to verify the work.\n\nWriting the task specification... I need to anchor each task requirement to a specific SRD requirement ID. Since I don't have the exact SRD text for srd009-du, I'll write reasonable descriptions that reference the requirement IDs and let the stitch agent fill in the details. Looking at the planning constitution example, I should include \"per srd009-du\" with a brief description of what to implement.\n\nFor the R1 group covering basic functionality, I'm mapping out the core disk usage features: recursive traversal with summation, the default tab-separated output format, block size handling with 512-byte defaults, hard link deduplication, and stdin handling. The R2 group will cover the various command-line flags. Now I'm writing out the full YAML structure with clean, meaningful descriptions for each task.\n\nWriting YAML task definitions... Looking at the design decisions and acceptance criteria for the du utility implementation. I need to set up the SIGPIPE handler at the start of main following the shared protocol, use Lstat to avoid following symlinks, and leverage the standard flag package for parsing options. The acceptance criteria confirm I need successful compilation, clean vet output, proper recursive disk usage reporting matching GNU du's format, and support for the core requirements around block size calculation and hard link deduplication.\n\nNow moving into the next phase which covers size display options and total mode, implementing requirements around exit codes, summarize mode with the -s flag, and additional size formatting options. Looking at the next task, I need to add traversal control and exclusion pattern support to the du implementation, including max depth limiting, one-filesystem mode, and other related features from the requirements specification. Looking at the next thinking, I can see it's continuing the acceptance criteria and moving into a new task about output formatting. Let me complete the previous thought and transition naturally:\n\n-system) not crossing mount points\n      - id: R3\n        text: Implement srd009-du R2.6 -- dereference symlinks (-L/--dereference) following symbolic links\n      - id: R4\n        text: Implement srd009-du R2.7 -- exclude patterns (--exclude) skipping matching files\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: Track filesystem device ID from pkg/sys.FileInfo.Dev to implement -x\n      - id: D2\n        text: Use filepath.Match for --exclude pattern matching\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: go build -o bin/du ./cmd/du/ succeeds\n      - id: AC2\n        text: go vet ./cmd/du/ passes\n      - id: AC3\n        text: du -d N limits output to depth N\n      - id: AC4\n        text: du -x stays on a single filesystem\n\nNow moving into the next phase where I need to handle output formatting and human-readable sizes, including apparent size reporting and other display options from the requirements. I'm realizing I need to be more careful about my requirement descriptions—they're educated guesses about what the SRD actually specifies, and if they contradict the real spec, the stitch agent could get confused. I should make these descriptions more generic and explicitly defer to the SRD as the source of truth. I'm also concerned about the pkg/format dependency I referenced for task 3—if that package doesn't exist, the stitch agent won't be able to import it, so I need to verify that or find an alternative approach. I'm verifying that my design decisions are grounded in the SRD and architecture rather than invented—the SIGPIPE handler comes from shared_protocols, the Lstat function from component_dependencies, and the flag package is standard Go practice. My acceptance criteria like \"go build succeeds\" and \"go vet passes\" are verifiable, though I should make sure the du output check is similarly unambiguous.\n\nSince all four tasks modify main.go, they need to run sequentially to avoid conflicts, so I'll keep the chain as 0→1→2→3. Now I'm working on the YAML structure, being careful with formatting.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":n